ground_truth_witness:
The pivotal step asserts the implicit lemma "if the interior part G-bar is 3-colourable and the boundary part dG is 3-colourable, then G is 3-colourable." That lemma is false, and exhaustive search found minimum-size witnesses under every reasonable reading of "two parts".

READING A (vertex split: dG = induced boundary CYCLE on S, G-bar = induced interior graph on V\S) — witness G = K4 on V = {0,1,2,3}, E = {01,02,03,12,13,23}:
  boundary S = {0,1,2}, dG = G[S] = triangle 01,02,12, chi(dG) = 3 (exactly the hypothesis chi(dG)=3 of the verbatim sentence);
  interior I = {3}, G-bar = G[I] = single vertex, no edges, chi(G-bar) = 1 <= 3;
  yet chi(G) = chi(K4) = 4.
  Claim says: chi(dG)=3 and chi(G-bar)<=3 => chi(G) <= 3; here chi(G) = 4 > 3.

READING B (edge decomposition, parts may share vertices: E(G) = E(G-bar) u E(dG)) — witness G = K4 again with E(G-bar) = {02,03,12,13,23} (chi = 3) and E(dG) = {01} (chi = 2), while chi(G) = 4 > 3.

READING B' (dG literally a spanning cycle) — G = K4, dG = 4-cycle 0-2-1-3-0 (chi = 2), G-bar = the two diagonals {01, 23} (chi = 2), chi(G) = 4 > 3.

READING B'' (strictest form of the antecedent: dG a spanning cycle with chi(dG) = 3 EXACTLY) — n = 5, E(G) = {01,02,03,04,12,13,14,23} (8 edges):
  dG = 5-cycle 0-3-2-1-4-0 (edges 03,32,21,14,40), chi(dG) = 3 exactly;
  G-bar = interior edges {01,02,13}, chi(G-bar) = 2 <= 3;
  but G contains K4 on {0,1,2,3} (edges 01,02,03,12,13,23) so chi(G) = 4 > 3.

In every case both parts satisfy the hypotheses used by the proof and the conclusion "G is 3-colourable" fails. All chromatic numbers were recomputed from scratch by exact integer backtracking.
